2026-02-10 21:22:29,076 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Key Takeaways

- The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) calculates the expected return of an asset by assessing its risk compared to the overall market.
- CAPM considers the risk-free rate, asset beta, and market risk premium to determine the expected rate of return.
- Despite criticisms of its assumptions, CAPM is widely used for its simplicity and comparative insights in investment evaluations.
- CAPM suggests that investors can only achieve higher returns by taking on increased risk, aligning with principles from modern portfolio theory.
- Alternatives to CAPM, like the Fama-French model, offer additional factors to better account for variations in asset returns. is relevant
2026-02-10 21:22:29,965 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 21:22:29,969 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document What Are Some Alternatives to the CAPM?

Because of its criticisms, several alternative models to the capital asset pricing model (CAPM) have been developed to understand the relationship between risk and reward in investments. One of these is arbitrage pricing theory (APT), a model that looks at multiple factors, grouped into macroeconomic or company-specific factors. Another is the Fama-French 3-factor model, which expands on CAPM by adding company-size risk and value risk factors to the market risk factors.

In 2015, Fama and French adapted their model to include five factors. Along with the original three factors, the new model adds the concept that companies reporting higher future earnings have higher returns in the stock market, a factor referred to as profitability. The fifth factor, referred to as “investment,” relates to the concept of internal investment and returns, suggesting that companies directing profit toward major growth projects are likely to experience losses in the stock market. is NOT relevant, dump it.
