2026-02-10 21:23:03,759 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document What Is the Gordon Growth Model (GGM)?

The Gordon Growth Model (GGM), a crucial tool in financial valuation, helps in determining a stock's intrinsic value based on expected future dividends growing at a constant rate. As a straightforward variant of the dividend discount model (DDM), it is particularly valuable for analyzing companies with stable dividend growth.

The model discounts the infinite series of future dividends to present value, offering investors insights into whether a stock is undervalued or overvalued relative to its current market price. By using dividends per share, growth rates, and the required rate of return as inputs, GGM streamlines the evaluation of a stock's fair value, especially for companies with predictable dividend patterns. is relevant
2026-02-10 21:23:04,781 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 21:23:04,788 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Warning

The Gordon Growth Model is only useful for valuing companies that regularly pay dividends. It may be unreliable for growth stocks, as these companies often reinvest profits instead of distributing them as dividends. is relevant
2026-02-10 21:23:05,907 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 21:23:05,911 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Challenges and Limitations of the Gordon Growth Model

The main limitation of the Gordon growth model lies in its assumption of constant growth in dividends per share. It is very rare for companies to show constant growth in their dividends due to business cycles and unexpected financial difficulties or successes. This limits the model to firms with stable growth rates.

The second issue occurs with the relationship between the discount factor and the growth rate used in the model. If the required rate of return is less than the growth rate of dividends per share, the result is a negative value, rendering the model worthless. If the required return rate equals the growth rate, the value per share goes to infinity. is relevant
2026-02-10 21:23:06,722 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 21:23:06,725 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Understanding the Dividend Discount Model: Formula, Examples, and Pitfalls is relevant
